(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-glaucoma-diagnostics-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Glaucoma & Diagnostic Agents Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is more than one agent often needed to control intraocular pressure?","choices":["Targeting production and outflow together","Tolerance develops to every single agent within weeks","Each agent can only be used at half dose","Single agents are contraindicated in open angle disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Combinations are described as synergistic for exactly that reason, and they also cut the number of drops a patient has to manage, which helps compliance. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 64 and 73"},{"q":"A patient is already on timolol and needs a second agent. Which combination product makes sense, and what is the general advantage of combinations?","choices":["A second beta blocker, because the effect is additive","Brimonidine with timolol, targeting both mechanisms","Dorzolamide with timolol, because both increase outflow","Pilocarpine alone, because miotics are best tolerated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Given the drug someone is already on, the question is what second-line agent helps most. Adding an alpha agonist to a beta blocker hits both production and outflow, and fewer drops improves compliance. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Apraclonidine is highly ionised at physiological pH while brimonidine is more lipophilic. What is the practical consequence?","choices":["Ionisation increases corneal penetration and local potency","Lipophilicity prevents systemic absorption","Ionisation limits blood-brain barrier penetration","The difference is purely one of shelf stability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A charged molecule crosses membranes poorly, so it stays where it is put. Brimonidine, being more lipophilic, is also the one with less allergic conjunctivitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What distinguishes angle closure glaucoma from open angle glaucoma?","choices":["Increased aqueous production with a gradual course","Optic nerve damage at a normal intraocular pressure","Blockage of the drainage canal, with acute pain","Raised pressure without any optic nerve damage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Open angle comes from increased production or reduced drainage and damages the nerve over time; angle closure is a mechanical blockage that presents acutely and painfully. Drug therapy targets open angle disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"In which patients are ophthalmic alpha-2 agonists contraindicated, and why?","choices":["Patients with asthma, because of bronchoconstriction","Patients with sulfonamide allergy","Patients over 65, because of falls","Children under two, from apnoea risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A hard age contraindication, and the mechanism is the same central alpha-2 effect that makes imidazoline ingestion dangerous in toddlers. Worth holding alongside that question. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A patient using latanoprost thinks two drops a night will lower the pressure further. What should they be told?","choices":["Two drops a night is acceptable if the pressure is not at target","It is harmless but wasteful","It should be split to morning and evening instead","Exceeding once daily dosing inhibits the pressure-lowering effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is counter-intuitive and therefore a good question: more drug does not mean more effect, it means less. Once daily is the maximum as well as the minimum. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How do dorzolamide and brinzolamide lower pressure?","choices":["They increase outflow through the trabecular meshwork","They block beta receptors in the ciliary body","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary epithelium","They activate muscarinic receptors on the ciliary muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Less bicarbonate means less fluid follows it, so aqueous production falls. It is a production-side drug, like the beta blockers but by a different route. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Glaucoma drugs work by one of two strategies. What are they?","choices":["Decreasing aqueous production or reducing episcleral venous pressure","Increasing outflow or reducing optic nerve metabolic demand","Dilating the pupil or constricting the ciliary muscle","Reducing production or increasing outflow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every agent in this section sorts into one or the other, and some do both. Sorting a drug by which side it is on is the fastest way to remember the whole class list. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which patients with raised pressure are treated, and which are monitored?","choices":["Treat those with risk factors; monitor those without","All patients with pressure above 21 mmHg are treated","Only those who already have visual field loss are treated","Treatment is started only once both eyes are affected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Raised pressure alone is not automatically a prescription. Risk factors decide, and in their absence you watch for the change that would make it a disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"For which procedures are topical ocular anaesthetics indicated?","choices":["Fundoscopy and pupil assessment","Treatment of corneal abrasion pain at home","Tonometry, foreign body removal, corneal surgery","Intravitreal injection only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three need the surface insensate but not the eye immobilised. That also explains why tonometry cannot simply be done on any patient without preparation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which classes reduce aqueous PRODUCTION?","choices":["Prostaglandins, beta blockers and cholinergic agonists","Alpha agonists, beta blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Cholinergic agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors only","Prostaglandins alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The alpha agonists sit on both sides of the list, which is the part most easily got wrong — they reduce production AND increase outflow. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How do atropine, cyclopentolate and tropicamide produce mydriasis?","choices":["They competitively block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors","They activate alpha-1 adrenergic receptors","They activate muscarinic receptors on the iris dilator","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blocking muscarinic receptors is the exact opposite of pilocarpine's action, which is why one dilates and the other constricts. They are used diagnostically and in uveitis to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How do ophthalmic alpha-2 agonists lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["They block beta receptors in the ciliary epithelium","They reduce production and increase outflow","They contract the ciliary muscle to open the trabecular meshwork","They analogue prostaglandin F2-alpha at prostaglandin receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They work on both sides of the equation, which is why they appear in both lists — decreased production and increased outflow. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"How do ophthalmic beta blockers lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["They block beta receptors in the ciliary epithelium","They increase outflow through the trabecular meshwork","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary epithelium","They contract the ciliary muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Less catecholamine activation means less cyclic AMP, and less aqueous is made. They act on the production side, not on drainage. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the general target for pressure reduction in glaucoma therapy?","choices":["A pressure below 10 mmHg in every patient","A 50 per cent reduction regardless of starting pressure","Any reduction at all, however small","A 20 to 30 per cent reduction in intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The goal is expressed as a proportion rather than an absolute number, which matches the stated aim: prevent progressive optic nerve damage with the least risk and fewest side effects. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which classes are named as reasonable places to START glaucoma therapy?","choices":["A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor or a cholinergic agonist","A prostaglandin analogue or a beta blocker","A combination product from the outset","A cholinergic agonist alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Either is a reasonable opening move, though the prostaglandins are described as first line and the most commonly used, with beta blockers second line. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"}]};
